<Project>

  <PropertyGroup>
    <LangVersion>12.0</LangVersion>
    <Nullable>enable</Nullable>
    <ImplicitUsings>enable</ImplicitUsings>
    <TreatWarningsAsErrors>true</TreatWarningsAsErrors>
    <EnforceCodeStyleInBuild>true</EnforceCodeStyleInBuild>
    <InvariantGlobalization>false</InvariantGlobalization>
    <Product>Loadstar</Product>
    <Company>Loadstar</Company>
    <!--
      The in-development version, and it must always be a MINOR ahead of the last release.

      CI builds rolling installers from `main` as <major>.<minor>.<run_number>, so this line owns a
      whole patch range that fills up as pushes land. A release tag then takes the NEXT minor and
      this value moves one further on, which keeps two rules true at once: every rolling build
      outranks the release before it, and no tag can ever collide with a rolling build that already
      shipped under the same number. MSI upgrade detection is the reason it matters — two different
      installers carrying one version is the failure that has no clean recovery on the user's machine.

      So: released 0.2.0 from the 0.1.x line, development moves to 0.3.x, the next release is v0.4.0.
      Releases land on even minors purely as a consequence; nothing depends on the parity itself.

      History, as a worked example of the rule: 0.1.x development -> released v0.2.0 -> 0.3.x
      development, which reached 0.3.31 -> released v0.4.0 -> 0.5.x development, which reached 0.5.45
      -> released v0.6.0 -> 0.7.x development, which reached 0.7.47 -> released v0.8.0 -> 0.9.x
      development, which reached 0.9.49 -> released v0.10.0 -> 0.11.x development, which reached 0.11.55
      -> released v0.12.0 -> 0.13.x development, which reached 0.13.57 -> released v0.14.0 -> 0.15.x
      development, which reached 0.15.58 -> released v0.16.0 -> 0.17.x development, which reached 0.17.60
      -> released v0.18.0 -> 0.19.x development, which reached 0.19.61 -> released v0.20.0 -> 0.21.x
      development, which reached 0.21.62 -> released v0.22.0 -> 0.23.x development, which reached 0.23.63
      -> released v0.24.0 -> 0.25.x development -> released v0.26.0 -> 0.27.x development, which is this
      line.
      A tagged build ignores this value entirely (CI passes -p:Version from the tag), so the only job
      it has is to keep rolling builds ahead of the last release and clear of the next one.
    -->
    <Version>0.27.0</Version>
  </PropertyGroup>

</Project>
